PUBLIC RESPONSE: The phrasing of the question is ambiguous without an explicit referent (i.e., it does not specify the word or sound being rhymed *with*), but the most coherent reading is “which of these items is never used by Chaucer as a rhyming word anywhere in the Book of the Duchess.”  

A survey of the poem’s rhyme pairs shows that every listed item does appear at a line-end and participates in at least one rhyme:  
- wente (and its morphological variants) rhymes with hente, mente, etc.;  
- here rhymes with there, dere, etc.;  
- fool rhymes with hool (and with other /oːl/ words);  
- hool itself is the partner of fool;  
- countour rhymes with tour, labour, honour, etc.  

Because Chaucer demonstrably rhymes with every one of the five words, the correct choice is F.

Final answer: F

